; dd2d9 configuration
; Global location: C:\windows\dd2d9.ini  (in a Wine prefix: drive_c/windows/dd2d9.ini)
; Also read, in order (later wins): <ddraw.dll dir>\dd2d9.ini, <game dir>\dd2d9.ini,
; and the file named by the DD2D9_INI environment variable. DD2D9_LOG overrides "log".
;
; Keys outside a section, in [dd2d9] and in [default] apply to every game; a [game.exe]
; section overrides them for that executable only (case-insensitive).
; The defaults behave like the original DirectDraw.

[default]
; What exclusive fullscreen games get:
;   switch     - change the display mode, as DirectDraw does (default). If the monitor
;                lacks the mode (320x200, 320x240...), falls back to borderless.
;   window     - no mode change: a window the size of the game's mode, centered
;   borderless - no mode change: a borderless window covering the monitor, image scaled
display=switch

; Scaling when the image doesn't fit 1:1 (window resized, borderless, windowed games):
;   aspect (default), fill, integer
stretch=aspect

; Scaling filter:
;   sharp  - (default, also bilinear-sharp) each pixel a flat block, blended with its
;            neighbour only over one screen pixel at the edge; crisp and even at any scale
;   point  - sharp pixels, but uneven at non-integer scales (the default before 1.5.0)
;   linear - smooth, but blurry
filter=sharp

; Vertical sync:
;   auto - the game decides: Flip() waits for vblank unless the game passes DDFLIP_NOVSYNC,
;          as DirectDraw does (default); a Direct3D game that keeps flipping without vsync is
;          presented without waiting too (may tear, as it asked)
;   on   - every Flip() waits for vblank
;   off  - never wait (uncapped, may tear; still at most two presents a refresh: more
;          wouldn't be shown, and their work would slow the game)
; On Windows with the desktop compositor (Windows 8 and later; 7 with Aero), dd2d9 waits
; itself, one frame per refresh, rather than Direct3D 9 (smoother; less CPU on some
; drivers). Under Wine, or without the compositor, Direct3D 9's vsync waits.
vsync=auto

; Direct3D (3D games):
;   auto - dd2d9's own Direct3D 3-7, drawn through Direct3D 9 (default); on is the same
;   off  - no Direct3D at all: games use their 2D or software renderers
d3d=auto

; Which d3d9.dll dd2d9 draws and presents through:
;   system - Windows' own, even when the game folder holds another one (DXVK's).
;            Presenting through DXVK's Vulkan in a window that covers the screen
;            can let the driver take over the display and switch it back to the desktop
;            resolution, which drops games out of fullscreen (default)
;   game   - the one the game loads (the game folder's, if there is one)
d3d9=system

; Compatibility: what the game is told about the machine, as a PC of its era would answer.
; Memory in MB (GlobalMemoryStatus). Windows tells programs that aren't large-address-aware
; there are 2 GB of memory and 4 GB of page file, and games of the era overflow adding to
; that and say there isn't enough memory. 0: the real values. At most 2047.
ram=1024
; Video memory in MB that DirectDraw reports (GetCaps, GetAvailableVidMem): 1 to 2047.
; Try 128 or 256 if a game complains about it.
vram=512
; Processor clock in MHz in the registry (~MHz): 2000 (default), as fast as the PCs of the
; era got. Games that time the processor themselves (RDTSC) still see the real one. A game
; that times itself with the registry's figure runs at the wrong speed: set 0 (the real
; clock) for it.
cpu=2000
; Processors the game runs on and is told about (GetSystemInfo): 1 (default), as the PCs of
; the era had; games of the era can crash, stutter or run at the wrong speed on several.
; One logical processor per physical core is taken, the first core last (it handles most of
; the system's interrupts). dd2d9's own presenting thread shares them. 0: all.
cpus=1
; The game's heap memory (HeapAlloc) comes zeroed: on (default), as fresh memory often was on
; Windows 9x, where games were tested; today's heap hands back blocks other code freed, with
; their old contents (Daytona USA Deluxe crashed on them as its 3D device came up). off: as
; Windows gives it, for a game that allocates a lot and needs none of this.
heapzero=on
; A critical section the game releases goes to the thread waiting for it: on (default), as on
; Windows up to XP. Since Vista the waiter is only woken, and a thread that releases a section
; and takes it again at once nearly always gets it (KOF 99 Evolution's intro stayed black:
; its decoder hardly ever got the section its main thread lets go of once a frame). off: as
; Windows has it now.
fairlocks=on
; The desktop's colour depth DirectDraw reports before the game sets its own mode: 32
; (default), 24 or 16. Some games refuse to start unless the desktop is 16-bit, even ones
; that then switch to their own mode (Moto Racer: "16 bit screen mode required"): set 16 for
; them, best in their own section ([game.exe]). A windowed game then draws in 16 bits, as on
; a 16-bit desktop; dd2d9 shows it either way.
; Also what GDI (GetDeviceCaps) and EnumDisplaySettings report, for games that check there:
; the game's own display mode's depth while it has one, else this.
desktopbpp=32
; A Lock of part of a surface counts as a change to all of it: off (default) or on. For a
; game that locks a small rectangle and writes outside it (XP let it): with antialiasing or
; Direct3D drawing into that surface, or on the screen, those writes would be left out.
; Costs a whole-surface copy per lock.
lockwhole=off

; Z buffers DirectDraw can read and write (Lock, Blt): on (default) where the GPU has the
; depth format for it and antialiasing is off; off: on the GPU only, as before 1.0.3 (a
; game that locks its Z buffer gets what DirectDraw itself last wrote there). For a driver
; that draws wrongly with it.
zaccess=on

; Overlays (video players, some games' movies): on (default) offers overlay surfaces, laid
; over the screen's image when shown, as the hardware of the era did; off reports no overlay
; hardware, as before 1.1.0 (games then blit their video instead).
videooverlay=on

; 8-bit palettized textures for Direct3D 3-7: on (default) offers them, as most 3D cards of
; the time did (dd2d9 converts them through their palette); off leaves them out of the
; formats a game is offered, as Matrox's G200 and G400 and today's drivers do. Some games
; pick their textures by it: N.I.C.E. 2 loads its 256-colour textures when they are
; offered and its 16-bit ones otherwise (off for those). A game that asks for one anyway
; still gets it.
palettizedtextures=on

; Frame-rate limit: 0 (off, default) or the most frames per second. For games that run too
; fast without vsync or busy a whole core. Paced at Flip, and at blits covering half the
; primary or more (games that show frames that way).
fpslimit=0

; Direct3D in 32 bits for 16-bit games: their render targets are drawn in 32 bits on the
; GPU (blending, fog and gradients without 16-bit banding) and converted when the game
; reads them. off (default) / on
render32=off
; 24-bit Z buffers for games that ask for 16-bit ones: less z-fighting (flickering where
; surfaces meet) in the distance. off (default) / on
depth24=off

; Antialiasing (MSAA): 0 (off, default), 2, 4 or 8 samples per pixel, at most what the GPU
; can. Render targets are drawn in 32 bits and Z buffers in 24 then. 2D a game writes into
; the back buffer between its 3D draws is kept; each time that happens costs a copy.
; Cut-out sprites (trees) drawn within their texture are addressed clamped meanwhile, so
; their edges don't pick up a line from the texture's other side. A game reading or
; copying its Z buffer through DirectDraw (rare) finds only what DirectDraw wrote there.
antialiasing=0
; With antialiasing, 2D drawn through Direct3D (HUDs, menus, text: screen-aligned, drawn
; with the depth test off, or always passing without writing depth) is drawn without it,
; pixel-sharp: off (default). on: antialiased like the 3D (its edges come out soft).
antialias2d=off

; Overlays (light maps, decals: passes that don't write depth, or that add or multiply
; colours; plain alpha blending doesn't count) pulled forward
; one step of the depth precision the game asked for. They differ from the surface under
; them only by rounding, which the 16-bit depth buffers of the era rounded the same way;
; deeper ones show it as hatched z-fighting (NFS III's headlights on the road), plus more
; where the surface is seen at a grazing angle. on (one 16-bit step), off, or a number of
; steps for games that still flicker (try 2, 4, 8: the larger, the more an overlay can show
; through things just in front of it)
overlaybias=on

; W buffering, which most cards of the era had and today's don't:
;   on    - offered to games, and emulated (default): depth from 1/w, so polygons in the same
;           plane agree however they are cut (NFS III's headlights and light maps on the road
;           z-fight without it); light maps and decals laid on top get a little slack
;   force - Z buffering of pre-transformed vertices treated the same, for games that draw
;           their own depth with the same trouble without asking for W buffering (breaks
;           games whose 1/w doesn't follow their depth: NFS III's car turns black)
;   off   - not offered
wbuffer=on

; Texture filtering: game (default) as the game sets it; linear: point-filtered textures
; drawn bilinear (blocky textures smoothed). Cut-outs (colour-keyed or with alpha) keep the
; game's filtering: their transparent texels would blend into the edges.
texfilter=game

; Mipmaps for textures the game gave none (most of DX7 and older): the GPU makes the smaller
; levels and filters with them, so textures don't shimmer and crawl in the distance. Softer
; than the hardware of the era showed. Cut-outs (colour-keyed or with an alpha channel) are
; left alone: their transparent texels would fringe the smaller levels. off (default) / on
mipmaps=off

; Anisotropic filtering: 0 (off, default), 2, 4, 8 or 16 (at most what the GPU can).
; Textures seen at a grazing angle (floors, roads, walls) stay sharp instead of blurring.
; Only where the game filters smoothly (linear): point-filtered textures stay as they are,
; and so do cut-outs (colour-keyed or with alpha: trees, sprites), which it would fringe.
; It works on mipmapped textures: for games that have none, together with mipmaps=on.
anisotropy=0

; Alternate pixel centre, as ATI's and Nvidia's old drivers offered: Direct3D drawn half a
; pixel up and left. For games that show lines or seams at the edges of textures, mostly in
; 2D drawn through Direct3D (menus, HUDs, sprites). off (default) / on
alternatepixelcenter=off

; Diagnostic: every ~10 seconds, one whole frame's Direct3D draws in the log (their depth,
; blend and alpha-test states, texture, and depth values). off (default) / on
;drawtrace=on

; Logging: 0 off (default), 1 info, 2 trace (every blit, lock, flip and palette change -
; very verbose, for diagnosing rendering problems).
; With log=1 or more, calls dd2d9 doesn't support are logged too ("stub: ...").
log=0
; Default log file: dd2d9.log next to the game's exe. If that folder isn't writable, dd2d9
; falls back to the current directory, then %TEMP%. A relative path is relative to the
; game's folder. The environment variable DD2D9_LOG=<path> also sets it.
;logfile=Z:\tmp\dd2d9.log

; Per-game example:
;[claw.exe]
;log=2
;logfile=Z:\tmp\claw-dd2d9.log
